      'Before ACES, different cameras, and even different firmware versions of the same camera, recorded color in their own proprietary color spaces and gammas, meaning colorists had to individually manage conversions for every source before combining footage in a single grade, with results that weren\'t always reproducible across facilities or years later during a remaster. ACES defines a large, device-independent working color space plus standardized Input and Output Device Transforms for specific cameras and displays, so footage from an Alexa, a RED, and an iPhone can be brought into the same color-managed pipeline and output correctly to a cinema DCP, a broadcast HDR master, or a future format not yet invented. Adoption grew through the 2010s as productions increasingly mixed camera systems and needed archival masters that would remain viable for future remastering. Trivia: ACES uses an unusually wide color space, AP0, that includes far more colors than any camera or display can actually capture or reproduce, specifically to future-proof the standard.',
