      'DLP Cinema projectors, dominant since their commercial rollout in the early 2000s, use a digital micromirror device: a semiconductor chip bearing millions of microscopic mirrors, each corresponding to one pixel, that tilt thousands of times per second between an \'on\' position reflecting light through the lens and an \'off\' position reflecting it away, with the proportion of on-time per mirror controlling perceived brightness. This differs fundamentally from LCD/LCOS projection, which modulates light by rotating its polarization through a liquid crystal layer rather than physically tilting mirrors, and from later laser-illuminated projectors, which still typically use the same DMD imaging chip but replace the xenon lamp with laser diodes or phosphor-wheel laser modules for brighter, more color-accurate illumination. Barco, Christie, and NEC are the three companies licensed to build DLP Cinema projectors using TI\'s chip. Trivia: a single cinema DMD chip contains well over two million mirrors, each smaller than a human red blood cell.',
