      'Dolby\'s Atmos cinema specification allows up to 128 simultaneous audio elements delivered to a theater, combining traditional bed channels functioning similarly to a standard 7.1 or 9.1 layout with dynamically positioned objects that the in-theater renderer maps onto whatever speaker configuration that auditorium has installed, from a modest multiplex screen up to a flagship premium auditorium with dozens of speakers including overhead arrays. This object-based approach lets the exact same Atmos master scale correctly across wildly different room sizes and speaker counts without a separate mix for each configuration, unlike the fixed-channel-count approach of earlier formats like Dolby Digital 5.1 or DTS. Because the number is a hard specification limit, mixers must carefully manage element budgets on dense action or music sequences, sometimes collapsing less important sounds into bed channels rather than keeping them as discrete objects. Trivia: consumer Dolby Atmos in home AV receivers uses the same object-based philosophy but is delivered through Dolby Digital Plus or TrueHD as a transport layer, with far fewer physical speakers typically available to render the height information.',
