      'By Avatar\'s production, Weta Digital\'s render farm, reported at roughly 40,000 processor cores across thousands of blade servers, ran on Linux, reflecting the broader VFX industry\'s shift away from proprietary Unix workstations like SGI\'s IRIX or Sun\'s Solaris toward cheap, powerful, flexible Linux-based commodity hardware that had taken hold industry-wide through the 2000s. This shift, driven by the collapsing cost-per-flop of PC-class hardware and the maturity of Linux rendering pipelines, let studios scale render capacity far beyond what proprietary Unix hardware of the 1990s could economically support, directly enabling the vastly more complex CG environments and characters that would have been computationally impractical a decade earlier. Windows has seen far more limited adoption in back-end render farm infrastructure historically, more common on artist workstations for specific software than as the render farm operating system itself. Trivia: Weta\'s Avatar render farm reportedly generated enough data that the studio\'s storage and data-management challenges were themselves a significant engineering effort separate from the rendering computation itself.',
