      'Gaussian splatting, introduced in a widely cited 2023 paper, represents a scene as millions of 3D Gaussian primitives, soft ellipsoidal blobs with position, scale, orientation, opacity, and view-dependent color, rather than a neural network function as in NeRF or a discrete voxel grid, and renders novel viewpoints extremely fast by directly \'splatting\' these Gaussians onto the screen rather than requiring the dense per-pixel neural network queries NeRF relies on. This combination of high visual quality and real-time rendering speed made it immediately attractive for virtual production, game engines, and VFX pipelines exploring photogrammetry-style capture, since it can be edited and relit more tractably than a NeRF\'s opaque neural weights, and several vendors began experimenting with it for LED-volume content and previs almost immediately after publication. Unlike a polygon mesh, a Gaussian splat scene has no explicit connected surface geometry, which is part of what makes traditional mesh-based editing and rigging tools awkward to apply to it directly. Trivia: the technique\'s speed made live Gaussian splat scene capture and viewing on consumer-grade hardware possible within roughly a year of the original paper\'s publication.',
